            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes values that grow by the same RATIO at every step, rather than by the same amount: 1 10 100 1000, each ten times the last. You give the first and last EXPONENTS - np.logspace 0 3 4 is 10 to the power of 0, 1, 2 and 3.\n\nEqual ratios are how pitch, loudness, brightness and many other perceptions work:\nan octave is a doubling wherever you start. Use it for frequency bands, a set of time scales (10 ms to 10 s), zoom levels, a sweep of filter cutoffs, or test values spanning many sizes.\n\nnp.geomspace does the same from the end values themselves, which is usually easier:\nnp.geomspace 1 1000 4 is the same as np.logspace 0 3 4.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.logspace <start exponent> <stop exponent> <steps>\nDefaults: 0, 3, 4 steps, base 10.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.logspace 0 7 8\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes the values and sends them.\n\nstart, stop (float boxes, also inlets):\nThe first and last exponents. Both ends are included.\n\nsteps (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values.\n\nbase (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat is raised to the exponents: 10 (the default), 2 for doublings, 2.71828 (e) for natural growth.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nlogspace out:\nThe values.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nMade at load: 10 to the powers 0 to 3, 1 10 100 1000; and with base 2, the powers 0 to 7:\n1 2 4 8 16 32 64 128.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.geomspace takes the end values instead of exponents. np.linspace spaces values by equal amounts. t.logspace is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Makes values that grow by the same RATIO at every step, rather than by the same amount: 1 10 100 1000, each ten times the last. You give the first and last EXPONENTS - np.logspace 0 3 4 is 10 to the power of 0, 1, 2 and 3.\n\nEqual ratios are how pitch, loudness, brightness and many other perceptions work:\nan octave is a doubling wherever you start. Use it for frequency bands, a set of time scales (10 ms to 10 s), zoom levels, a sweep of filter cutoffs, or test values spanning many sizes.\n\nnp.geomspace does the same from the end values themselves, which is usually easier:\nnp.geomspace 1 1000 4 is the same as np.logspace 0 3 4.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.logspace <start exponent> <stop exponent> <steps>\nDefaults: 0, 3, 4 steps, base 10.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.logspace 0 7 8\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes the values and sends them.\n\nstart, stop (float boxes, also inlets):\nThe first and last exponents. Both ends are included.\n\nsteps (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values.\n\nbase (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat is raised to the exponents: 10 (the default), 2 for doublings, 2.71828 (e) for natural growth.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nlogspace out:\nThe values.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nMade at load: 10 to the powers 0 to 3, 1 10 100 1000; and with base 2, the powers 0 to 7:\n1 2 4 8 16 32 64 128.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.geomspace takes the end values instead of exponents. np.linspace spaces values by equal amounts. t.logspace is the PyTorch version."
        },
